          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does COPD guidance describe about when to add an inhaled corticosteroid?",
          "answer": "The COPD guidance in this system describes adding an inhaled corticosteroid as a decision based on a blood test marker and past flare-ups. It does not give a numeric cutoff.\n\n**What the guidance says.** In the \"COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy\" document (Global respiratory strategy report, educational summary), people with a significant exacerbation history (flare-ups needing treatment) are placed on two long-acting bronchodilators. An inhaled corticosteroid is added where the **blood eosinophil count** is high, or where features of coexisting asthma are present. The guidance uses this marker because inhaled corticosteroids carry an increased risk of pneumonia in this group, so the benefit has to be identifiable before the drug is used. A separate general reference (\"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)\") says the same thing more briefly: inhaled corticosteroids are used where indicated by exacerbations or eosinophil count.\n\n**What I could not find.** The retrieved passages do not say what counts as a \"high\" eosinophil count, or how many exacerbations make a history \"significant.\" I have not filled these gaps from memory. To get the numbers, check the full guideline text or a clinician's source.\n\n**Where authorities differ.** The COPD guidance here carries no disagreement note, and none of the retrieved disagreements concern COPD. The disagreements returned were about asthma reliever choice, hypertension and influenza antivirals. So I cannot report a COPD-specific split between authorities from this material.\n\n**Strength of evidence.** The retrieved passages do not grade the evidence behind the eosinophil approach, so I cannot say how strong it is.\n\nA clinician can explain how these criteria apply to a specific person's test results and history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
